    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          iFIT is the reason a NordicTrack or ProForm console looks the way it does, and the reason
          the machine was priced the way it was. Whether it is worth paying for depends far less
          on the content than on how you actually train — and on a sum most buyers never do.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>iFIT is worth it if</strong> you are motivated by instructors, you will use
            classes or guided workouts several times a week, you like the machine changing speed
            and incline for you, and you have priced the membership into what the treadmill
            really costs. For that person it does something a plain console cannot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>iFIT is probably not worth it if</strong> you mostly set a speed and run, you
            watch your own content while you train, you would use it once a week at most, or the
            membership is the difference between a machine you can afford and one you cannot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Can you use a NordicTrack without iFIT?</strong> Yes. On the machines we are
            aware of, the treadmill runs in a manual mode with speed and incline control. What
            you lose beyond the classes varies by model and firmware, and on many current models
            the large touchscreen does considerably less without a membership. Check the specific
            model before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are still choosing a machine, our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            is the companion to this page, and covers the machines that work fully without any
            recurring fee.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-ifit-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What iFIT Actually Is</h2>
<p>
            iFIT is a subscription fitness platform run by iFIT Health &amp; Fitness, the company
            that also owns NordicTrack and ProForm. That ownership is the important detail. iFIT
            is not a third-party app that happens to work with these machines; the hardware and
            the subscription come from the same company, and the consoles are designed around the
            service.
          </p>
          <p>
            The platform delivers workouts to the touchscreen on the treadmill — and, depending on
            plan and device, to phones, tablets and televisions — with trainers leading sessions
            and the machine adjusting itself to match. It also covers other equipment in the same
            family: bikes, rowers, ellipticals and strength content, which matters for households
            that own more than one machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Commercially, the arrangement is familiar from other connected-fitness brands. The
            hardware is priced competitively for its specification, often with a trial membership
            bundled, and the ongoing membership is a large part of the business. That is not
            sinister, but it is worth understanding: the console is built to make the subscription
            attractive, and a buyer who does not subscribe is paying for screen and electronics
            they will use only partly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our brand guides to
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a> and
            <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm</a>
            cover the hardware side of both ranges.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-get" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What the Membership Gets You</h2>
<p>
            The content is the obvious part, but the feature that most owners describe as the
            reason they keep paying is the machine controlling itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Trainer-led classes.</strong> A large library of studio sessions — intervals,
            hill work, walking, recovery, running technique — led by instructors on screen. The
            appeal is the same as any class: someone else decides what you do next, and you only
            have to keep up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Automatic speed and incline.</strong> During an iFIT workout the treadmill
            changes speed and incline to match the session, without you touching the controls.
            On incline trainers with a wide range, this is a genuinely different experience from
            pressing buttons, and it removes the negotiation with yourself mid-session. You can
            usually override the changes if they are too much.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Global and scenic workouts.</strong> Filmed routes in locations around the
            world, with the incline following the terrain on screen. For people who find
            treadmill running dull, this is the feature most often cited as worth the money.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tracking and programmes.</strong> Workout history, progress over time,
            multi-week programmes and, depending on the plan, multiple user profiles. Family
            plans have historically been the way households with more than one regular user share
            a machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this changes the hardware. A membership does not make the motor stronger, the
            deck longer or the belt quieter; it changes how you use what you already have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="without-ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Can You Use a NordicTrack or ProForm Without iFIT?</h2>
<p>
            Yes — the treadmill does not stop being a treadmill. What it becomes without a
            membership is the question, and the honest answer is that it varies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What works on the machines we are aware of:</strong> a manual mode in which you
            start the belt, set speed and incline using the console or the quick-adjust buttons,
            and see basic metrics such as time, distance and speed. That is enough to walk or run
            on the machine indefinitely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What is often limited:</strong> on many current models, the large touchscreen
            is substantially an iFIT interface, so without a membership it shows a minimal
            manual-mode display rather than a full set of programmes. Workout history, built-in
            programmes and some display features may be reduced or absent. Older and cheaper
            models with button consoles often retain more onboard programmes, because they were
            never designed around the screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why we cannot give a definitive list:</strong> the behaviour differs between
            models, between model years sold under the same name, and between firmware versions.
            Firmware updates have changed what consoles allow over time, including how much of the
            underlying tablet owners can access. A description that was accurate for one model in
            one year may not hold for the next.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How to check before you buy:</strong> read the specific model's manual and
            support pages for what it calls manual mode, look for owner reviews describing use
            without a membership, and ask the retailer directly what the console does with no
            active plan. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">no-subscription guide</a>
            explains the three tiers of subscription dependence and how to tell which a machine
            sits in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What iFIT Costs — and Why You Should Check</h2>
<p>
            iFIT's prices have changed several times, and the plan line-up has been restructured
            more than once, so any figure on a review site — this one included — should be treated
            as a starting point for checking rather than a quote.
          </p>
          <p>
            Historically, plans have been priced in the region of $15 a month for an individual
            membership and around $39 a month for a family membership, with annual billing
            generally working out cheaper per month than paying monthly. At the time of writing,
            check iFIT's current plan page for the actual prices and what each plan includes,
            because both have moved and may move again.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two details are easy to miss. First, many machines are sold with a bundled trial or a
            prepaid membership period, and what happens when it ends — automatic renewal, and at
            which plan and price — is set out in the terms. Second, the plan that suits a
            household with several users is usually the dearer family tier, so a couple sharing a
            treadmill should price that tier, not the individual one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The important thing is not the exact monthly figure. It is that a monthly figure
            sounds small, and it is the wrong unit for a machine you expect to own for a decade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-run-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Five- and Ten-Year Arithmetic</h2>
<p>
            Treadmills are long-lived purchases. A well-maintained mid-range machine can be in use
            for many years, and our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            covers what to expect. Spread a membership over that life and the numbers change
            character.
          </p>
          <p>
            Using the historical figures above purely as illustration: at about $15 a month, a
            membership is roughly $180 a year, about $900 over five years and about $1,800 over
            ten. At about $39 a month it is roughly $470 a year, about $2,340 over five years and
            about $4,680 over ten. Annual billing reduces those somewhat; price rises increase
            them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set that against the hardware. A family plan kept for ten years can cost more than a
            treadmill in the $1,500 to $2,000 range, and an individual plan over the same period
            approaches the price of the machine itself. In other words, the "real" price of an
            iFIT treadmill you subscribe to is the hardware plus a second treadmill's worth of
            membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is not an argument against subscribing. A gym membership costs money every month
            too, and people pay it gladly when they use it. It is an argument for comparing
            machines on total cost rather than sticker price. A subscription-free treadmill at a
            higher price can be the cheaper machine to own, which is exactly the comparison our
            <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">spending guide</a>
            and
            <a href="/cheap-vs-premium-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cheap vs. premium comparison</a>
            encourage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Who iFIT Is Worth It For</h2>
<p>
            <strong>People who need someone else to set the session.</strong> If the hardest part
            of training is deciding what to do and then doing it, a trainer and a machine that
            adjusts itself remove both problems. This is the strongest case, and for these users
            the membership is often what turns a treadmill from furniture into equipment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>People who find treadmills boring.</strong> Scenic routes with matching
            incline, and classes with a running commentary, are the most effective answer to
            treadmill monotony that the hardware itself offers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Households with several users or several machines.</strong> A family plan
            shared across multiple people, or covering a bike and a treadmill, spreads the cost
            considerably. Per person, per session, it can compare well with gym or studio
            alternatives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline-trainer owners.</strong> On machines with a wide incline range, the
            automatic adjustments make the hardware feel noticeably more capable than pressing
            buttons does. Much of what makes those machines special is exposed through structured
            workouts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anyone replacing a class or gym membership.</strong> If iFIT genuinely
            replaces something you were already paying for, the comparison is with that cost, not
            with zero.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Who Should Skip It</h2>
<p>
            <strong>People who just run.</strong> If your sessions are "press start, set a speed,
            watch something, finish", you are paying for features you will not use. A
            subscription-free machine with a good deck and motor will serve you better per dollar.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>People who follow their own plan.</strong> Runners working from a coach's
            schedule or a training plan of their own usually want to set specific paces and
            inclines, not follow a class. Manual mode does that already.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>People who watch their own content.</strong> If the treadmill sits in front of
            a television or you train to podcasts, the screen is redundant, and on many models it
            cannot conveniently show other services anyway.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Occasional users.</strong> At once a week, the per-session cost of a membership
            is high, and the classes will be the first thing you stop doing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buyers at the top of their budget.</strong> If the hardware itself stretches
            you, a recurring fee on top is how a treadmill purchase becomes a regret. Buy the best
            subscription-free machine the budget allows; our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-2000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$2,000 guide</a>
            covers the range where most of these decisions are made.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Alternatives: Third-Party Apps and Bluetooth</h2>
<p>
            iFIT is not the only way to get guided or scenic workouts on a treadmill, though the
            alternatives come with caveats about compatibility.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Third-party apps.</strong> Services such as Zwift and Kinomap, and a range of
            class and coaching apps, offer virtual routes, structured workouts and classes on a
            phone, tablet or television. Most are subscriptions too, but they are not tied to one
            hardware brand, so they survive a change of treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bluetooth FTMS.</strong> Many treadmills broadcast speed, distance and
            sometimes incline using a Bluetooth standard known as FTMS (Fitness Machine Service),
            which compatible apps can read. A smaller number allow apps to control the machine as
            well. Support varies widely by brand, model and firmware, and machines built around a
            proprietary platform do not always offer it; check the specific model rather than
            assuming.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Footpods.</strong> Where a treadmill does not broadcast its data, a shoe-mounted
            footpod can supply pace to an app instead. Accuracy depends on calibration, but it is
            a widely used workaround and works on any treadmill, motorized or not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Free and low-cost options.</strong> A tablet on the console shelf running video
            workouts, a podcast, or a printed interval plan cost little or nothing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts guide</a>
            has structured sessions that need nothing but manual mode.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the real question is iFIT against the other large connected-fitness platform, our comparison of <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs. Peloton</a> sets out hardware lock-in, training feel and what each is like without the subscription.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Resale and the Long Tail</h2>
<p>
            A connected treadmill raises questions that a plain one does not, and they surface
            when you come to sell or keep the machine for many years.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Resale.</strong> Used iFIT machines sell, and a buyer who wants the service
            will simply start their own membership. But the console is part of the value, and a
            screen whose usefulness depends on a subscription is worth less to a buyer who does
            not want one. Before selling, remove the machine from your account and factory-reset
            the console as the manufacturer describes, so the next owner can register it. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs. used guide</a>
            covers what secondhand buyers look for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console life.</strong> Touchscreens and their software age faster than frames
            and motors. Over a long ownership the hardware may outlive the point at which its
            console receives updates, and nobody can promise how a given screen will be supported
            ten years from now. A machine whose manual mode is fully usable is better protected
            against that than one that depends on the screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Platform risk.</strong> Any subscription service can change its plans,
            features and prices. That is a general point about connected fitness rather than a
            prediction about iFIT, but it is a reason to value what the hardware does on its own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cancelling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Trials, Renewals and Cancelling</h2>
<p>
            Most of the frustration owners report with connected-fitness subscriptions comes from
            renewals they did not expect rather than from the service itself. A few habits avoid
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Note the trial end date</strong> the day the machine arrives. Bundled trials
            commonly convert to a paid plan automatically, and a reminder a week beforehand gives
            you time to decide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Read which plan the trial converts to.</strong> If it is the family tier and
            you train alone, change plan before it renews rather than after.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Know how to cancel before you need to.</strong> Check whether cancellation is
            handled in the account settings online, through the app, or by contacting support, and
            whether an annual plan is refundable part-way through. The terms at the time of
            purchase are what apply, so keep a copy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Try a month of manual mode deliberately.</strong> Before renewing, or after a
            trial ends, use the machine without the membership for a few weeks. If you barely
            notice, you have your answer; if you stop using the treadmill, you also have your
            answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-decision" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. How It Should Change What You Buy</h2>
<p>
            The most useful time to decide whether iFIT is worth it is before you buy the
            treadmill, because the answer changes which machine makes sense.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will subscribe,</strong> a NordicTrack or ProForm can be good value:
            the hardware is competitively specified for its price and the console is built to
            make the most of the service. Budget for the membership over the life of the machine,
            and choose the plan tier that matches your household.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will not,</strong> you are paying for a screen you will use only
            partly. Spend the money on a better deck, motor and warranty from a brand that does not
            depend on a subscription, and add a tablet or app later if you want content. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">no-subscription guide</a>
            covers those machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are unsure,</strong> buy a machine whose manual mode you would be happy
            with on its own, and treat any membership as a bonus you can cancel. That is the
            option least likely to leave you with either an unused subscription or an
            underused screen.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is iFIT worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For people who will use trainer-led classes or scenic workouts several times a week, and who like the machine adjusting speed and incline for them, often yes. For people who mostly set a speed and run, or would use it occasionally, usually no. Over five to ten years the membership can cost as much as the treadmill, so price it in before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does iFIT cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Prices change, so check iFIT's current plan page. Historically, plans have been priced in the region of $15 a month for an individual membership and around $39 a month for a family plan, with annual billing generally cheaper per month. Many machines also come with a trial or prepaid period that converts to a paid plan when it ends.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a NordicTrack without iFIT?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. On the machines we are aware of, the treadmill runs in a manual mode with speed and incline control and basic metrics. On many current models the touchscreen does considerably less without a membership, and what is available varies by model and firmware. Check the specific model's documentation and owner reviews before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use Zwift or other apps on a NordicTrack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model and firmware. Some treadmills broadcast data over Bluetooth that apps like Zwift and Kinomap can read, while others built around a proprietary platform do not. Where a machine does not broadcast its data, a shoe-mounted footpod can supply pace to the app instead. Check compatibility for the specific model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if I cancel my iFIT membership?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The treadmill keeps working in manual mode, but you lose the classes, automatic speed and incline adjustments, scenic workouts and, on many models, much of what the touchscreen displays. Check how cancellation is handled and whether annual plans are refundable in the terms you agreed to, and note any trial end date so it does not renew unexpectedly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill without a subscription better value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, if you will not use the classes. A subscription-free machine costs only its purchase price, while a membership over many years can add as much again. For people who will use the content regularly, the subscription can be worth it. The right comparison is total cost over the years you expect to own the machine, not the price at checkout.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With iFIT and Treadmill Subscriptions</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Nearly every regret about iFIT comes from one of these four, and none of them is about the content.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing treadmills on sticker price alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A machine that needs a membership to be fully useful costs its price plus the membership over the years you own it. Over five to ten years that can equal the hardware. Compare total cost, and a pricier subscription-free machine can turn out to be the cheaper one to own.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming manual mode is the same on every model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">What a NordicTrack or ProForm does without a membership varies by model, model year and firmware, and the same product name can behave differently from one year to the next. Check the specific model's documentation and recent owner reports rather than relying on a general description.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting a trial renew on the wrong plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bundled trials commonly convert automatically, sometimes to a family tier a single user does not need. Note the end date when the machine arrives, check which plan it converts to, and decide deliberately rather than discovering the charge on a statement.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the big screen and never subscribing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The touchscreen is a meaningful part of what you pay for on connected machines. If you already know you will not subscribe, that money is better spent on deck size, motor quality and warranty from a brand whose console does not depend on a membership.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

